+        <div id="journal-text" class="journal-content">
+          <p>
+            Every exceptional cup of coffee begins long before the beans ever reach our roastery drum. At The Daily Grind, our bean sourcing process is built on multi-year direct trade partnerships with smallholder farmers and cooperatives across the high-altitude growing regions of Yirgacheffe in Ethiopia, Huila in Colombia, and Huehuetenango in Guatemala. By bypassing traditional commodity middlemen, we pay growers at least 35% above Fair Trade baseline prices, ensuring that the families tending the trees can invest in regenerative soil practices, shade-canopy preservation, and fair wages for seasonal harvest workers.
+          </p>
+          <p>
+            High elevation—typically between 1,500 and 2,100 meters above sea level—plays a vital role in developing the complex sugars and vibrant acidity we look for in specialty Arabica varieties. Cooler mountain nights slow down the maturation of the coffee cherry, allowing the seed inside to absorb more nutrients and develop dense cellular structures. During harvest season, our partner farms rely exclusively on selective hand-picking, where skilled harvesters pass through the same rows multiple times over several weeks to gather only cherries at peak, deep-crimson ripeness.
+          </p>
+          <p>
+            Once harvested, the cherries undergo meticulous post-harvest processing tailored to their regional terroir. In Colombia and Guatemala, beans are depulped within hours of picking, fermented in clean mountain spring water to gently remove mucilage, and sun-dried on raised African beds for up to two weeks with hourly raking for uniform moisture levels. For our Ethiopian lots, we also select natural-process micro-lots where whole cherries dry intact under the sun, infusing the green coffee with unmistakable notes of wild blueberry, bergamot, and jasmine.
+          </p>
+          <p>
+            Before any harvest shipment is approved for export, our green-coffee buyers and certified Q-Graders conduct blind cupping evaluations alongside farm agronomists. Every lot must score 86 points or higher on the Specialty Coffee Association scale and pass strict moisture and water-activity checks. Once the hermetically sealed GrainPro bags arrive at our local roastery, we craft custom small-batch roast profiles for each origin to highlight its natural sweetness, aroma, and origin character—delivering full traceability from the farm gate straight to your morning cup.
+          </p>
+        </div>
+      </article>
+    </section>
